  "result": "If I had one magic-wand change, I wouldn't use it to end war, or poverty, or disease directly \u2014 tempting as those are.\n\nI'd make curiosity and empathy stronger than fear and pride.\n\nSpecifically: I'd give every person an instinct, when they're about to dismiss, dehumanize, or harm someone, to vividly and briefly *get* what life is like from inside that other person's head. Not agree with them. Not excuse them. Just understand \u2014 their fears, their history, what they're trying to protect, what it feels like to be on the receiving end.\n\nI pick that because so many of our biggest problems aren't really problems of resources or intelligence. We know how to grow enough food. We know how to prevent most early deaths. We know fossil fuels are warming the planet. We disagree, fiercely, about who matters, who's lying, who's to blame, and who should sacrifice.\n\nYou can't legislate or invent your way out of that if people can't hear each other.\n\nWith that one change, people would still disagree \u2014 and they should. Disagreement is how we learn. But they'd disagree differently:\n\nA politician would have a harder time selling a policy by turning a group into a caricature. A normal argument wouldn't spiral so fast, because \"you're evil/stupid\" would be replaced by \"oh, I see why that scares you.\" Scientific evidence would be less threatening to our identity, so updating our minds wouldn't feel like losing.\n\nAnd it wouldn't just fix conflict. Curiosity is the engine for everything good, too \u2014 art, science, friendship, falling in love with a place or an idea that isn't yours. Living in a world where everyone was a little more wonder-driven than tribe-driven would just be more *interesting* to live in.\n\nMy worry with bigger wishes \u2014 like \"make everyone happy\" or \"make everyone agree\" \u2014 is that they'd flatten what makes people human. I wouldn't want to erase difference. I'd just want us to be better at crossing it.\n\nWhat would your one change be \u2014 a material one, like ending scarcity, or a human-nature one like this?",
